section: tools
title: Client tools
about: |
  Rules of the tool that makes the entity, such as the provider, and of the lab. They are out of
  scope for strata-preflight, and they stay in the catalog so that each fault of the live runs has
  a row. A lab row with a check that a command on this machine can answer runs as a cloud check.
rules:
- id: strata-binary-stale
  title: The strata binary is newer than its sources
  engines:
  - all
  - lab
  tier: tool
  severity: warn
  status: seen
  what: |
    A release binary older than a source file runs old code. A script that you run directly does
    not build first, so a run can use a binary that lacks an option the script needs. `lib.sh`
    compares the file times and stops. Run `just build`.
  reproduce:
  - 'find src Cargo.toml Cargo.lock -type f -newer target/release/strata -print -quit   # a path means the binary is stale; run just build'
  fix:
    owner: lab
    text: Build before each run; `lib.sh` refuses a stale binary.
  evidence:
  - kind: source
    ref: examples/live/lib.sh
    note: '`find ... -newer "$strata"` over src, Cargo.toml, and Cargo.lock; a hit stops the script'
- id: tools-present
  title: Each tool of the lab is on the PATH
  engines:
  - all
  - lab
  tier: cloud
  severity: warn
  status: seen
  what: |
    A missing tool stops a lab run at the step that needs it. The list is the lab's: tofu, jq,
    just, helm, crane, oras, zarf, kubectl, gcloud, aws. The check prints each name that
    `command -v` cannot find. A company puts its own list in a probes file.
  check:
    kind: exec
    needs:
    - sh
    argv:
    - sh
    - -c
    - for t in tofu jq just helm crane oras zarf kubectl gcloud aws; do command -v $t >/dev/null || echo $t; done
    expr: |
      if .exec.exit != 0 then ["the tool check failed: \(.exec.stderr | tostring | .[:200])"]
      else [ (.exec.stdout | tostring) | split("\n")[] | select(length > 0) | "\(.) is not on the PATH" ] end
  reproduce:
  - for t in tofu jq just helm crane oras zarf kubectl gcloud aws; do command -v $t >/dev/null || echo "$t is missing"; done
  fix:
    owner: lab
    text: Check each tool at the start of a run, before the first step that needs one.
  evidence:
  - kind: source
    ref: examples/live/cluster.sh, examples/live/registry.sh
    note: each script checks its own tools with `command -v` and stops on the first missing one
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/eks/tool-missing.json
    clean: fixtures/eks/tools-present-clean.json
- id: cloud-cli-auth
  title: The cloud CLI has live credentials
  engines:
  - aws
  - eks
  - lab
  tier: cloud
  severity: warn
  status: seen
  what: |
    An SSO profile of the AWS CLI expires, and `gcloud` needs a new login after some hours. Each
    cloud check of this machine then fails or is not run, and the inventory cannot run. The check
    calls `aws sts get-caller-identity`. For GCP, run `gcloud auth print-access-token` by hand.
    A row of the lab; it runs wherever the AWS CLI is on the PATH.
  check:
    kind: exec
    needs:
    - aws
    argv:
    - aws
    - sts
    - get-caller-identity
    - --output
    - json
    expr: |
      if .exec.exit != 0 then ["aws sts get-caller-identity failed: \(.exec.stderr | tostring | .[:200]); the AWS CLI has no live credentials, log in again"]
      elif (.exec.stdout.Account // "") == "" then ["aws sts get-caller-identity gave no Account: \(.exec.stdout | tostring | .[:200])"]
      else [] end
  reproduce:
  - aws sts get-caller-identity
  - 'gcloud auth print-access-token > /dev/null && echo ''gcloud: logged in'''
  fix:
    owner: lab
    text: Check the credentials of each cloud CLI at the start of a run.
  evidence:
  - kind: source
    ref: docs/src/development/live-example.md Prerequisites
    note: the token expires, so a run after some hours needs the login again
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/eks/aws-token-expired.json
    clean: fixtures/eks/cloud-cli-auth-clean.json
